    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I've implemented `run_tool` in `opsbot/calls.py`, and all 7 tests pass (4 new, 3 existing).\n\n```python\nresult = await session.call_tool(name, arguments)\ntext = \"\\n\".join(block.text for block in result.content if isinstance(block, TextContent))\nif result.is_error:\n    raise ToolCallFailed(name, text)\nif \"structured_content\" in result.model_fields_set:\n    return result.structured_content\nreturn text\n```\n\nI checked the pinned `mcp==2.2.0` API with Context7 and in the installed source before writing it:\n\n- **Result format:** by default, `ClientSession.call_tool` returns a `CallToolResult` with `content`, `structured_content` and `is_error`. The server's error text is in `content`.\n- **Other content:** image and other non-text blocks are skipped, both in the returned text and in the error message.\n- **Checking for structured output:** in this version, structured output can be any JSON value, including `{}`, `0` or `[]`. A plain truthiness check would treat an empty value as missing and return the text instead. So I check whether the server actually sent the field.\n\n**Tests:** the new tests are in `tests/test_calls.py`. They connect a real `ClientSession` in memory to a low-level `mcp` `Server` and cover:\n- text blocks joined with non-text blocks skipped\n- structured output returned as-is\n- empty `{}` structured output returned rather than the text\n- `ToolCallFailed` raised with the right tool name and joined message\n\nOne limitation: the tests can't show what happens if a server explicitly sends `\"structuredContent\": null`, because the server side leaves that field out when it's `None`.\n\nNo dependencies were added. The tests use `anyio`'s pytest plugin, which is already installed with `mcp`."
